[32m+[m[32m<p>Your average U.S. talk show just looked like a mid-level podcast setup. I think most production companies realised they have a good stable of talent — let's have them present shows, and we have a ready-made cast of characters to guest those shows. Put them on YouTube, Spotify, and the like, bypass the broadcaster, and keep the income for yourself. While Joe Rogan is at the peak, I think what comes next is oversaturation. I remember when there were five terrestrial TV channels in the UK; now there are so many choices, and yet it can still feel like there is nothing on. With the advent of streaming services, everyone was happy to pay one company £9.99 a month for access to basically everything, and now there are so many streamers and paywalls that people just can't afford it, so don't watch anything. There are like 1,500 football podcasts, 3,000 comedians and their rotating guests, and all your fan-made content too.</p>[m[41m [m
blog/posts/podcast-blah-blah.html:167: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m<p>I don't fault the content — you can throw so much against a wall and you would be surprised at what sticks. The general public are genuinely fickle — they like what they like for no rhyme or reason. There are genuinely some good podcasts/vodcasts out there. We are in a sweet spot, particularly in the UK, where we can get these podcasts for free through places like YouTube, and they have paid-for extra content like "The Rest Is..." Goalhanger shows do. How long until you have to subscribe to listen to them all, though? If and when someone gets that bright idea — and especially if it works for one — they will all try it.</p>[m[41m [m
blog/posts/podcast-blah-blah.html:173: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m<p>Right now, my podcast app of choice is AntennaPod. I found it at the right time. A lot of the good podcast players were now charging for quite basic things or including features that felt irrelevant, like file storage, etc. I was becoming more disillusioned with tech in general, so I decided to see if there was any free open-source software I could find and maybe switch fully over to. The AntennaPod app was initially a bit buggy to begin with, but it has really been doing great with each passing update — people who clearly love podcasts looking to make a great app and not make money from it.</p>[m[41m [m
